<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sucre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1538">1538</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Chuquisaca, La Plata (Bolivia)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sucre is the constitutional capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, founded by the Spanish in 1538 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-plata">La Plata</a> and also called Chuquisaca. From 1559 it was the seat of the Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Charcas</a>, governing Upper Peru and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a>, and it had an archbishopric and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-san-francisco-xavier">University of San Francisco Xavier</a>. Bolivian independence was declared there in 1825, and it was renamed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-jose-de-sucre">Antonio José de Sucre</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sucre.jpg" alt="Sucre" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Parallelepiped09 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sucre,_Capital_de_Bolivia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sucre lies in south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> at about 2,790 m above sea level, in the inter-Andean valleys of the Eastern Cordillera, at the foot of the Sica Sica and Churuquella hills. The area marks the boundary between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> basin and the basin of the River Plate, and its climate is mild and dry for the altitude. The city has been known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Charcas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-plata">La Plata</a>, Chuquisaca and Sucre, and its white-painted colonial houses earned it the nickname La Ciudad Blanca, the White City.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the Spanish arrived the site was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> town called Chuquisaca, the provincial capital of the wamani of Charca, which Topa Inka Yupanqui had taken from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aymara">Aymara</a> kingdom that held the area. The name possibly comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> words meaning a heap of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. In 1538 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-pizarro">Hernando Pizarro</a> travelled there with the Emperor Paullu Inca and met Consara, the principal lord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Charcas</a>, who told him of the silver, gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> deposits of the region.</p><p>The Spanish city was founded on 30 November 1538 by Pedro Anzures, Marqués de Campo Redondo, as Ciudad de la Plata de la Nueva Toledo. In 1559 Philip II set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Charcas</a> there, with authority over what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>, south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>. It belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a> until 1776, then to the Viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>. An archbishopric was founded in 1609 and the university of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-xavier">Francis Xavier</a> in 1624.</p><p>On 25 May 1809 the bell of the Basilica of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> was rung to begin the Bolivian independence movement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-plata">La Plata</a> was proclaimed provisional capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Upper Peru</a> in July 1826, and on 12 July 1839 President José Miguel de Velasco named it capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> and renamed it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-jose-de-sucre">Antonio José de Sucre</a>. After the decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a>'s silver industry the seat of government moved to La Paz in 1898.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sucre is the constitutional capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> and the capital of the Chuquisaca Department; the Supreme Court sits there, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-paz">La Paz</a> is the seat of government. Its colonial and republican centre, laid out on a grid, became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1991. Landmarks include the House of Freedom, built in 1621, where the declaration of independence is kept, and the Metropolitan Cathedral, built between 1559 and 1712. Sucre remains the seat of the Roman Catholic Church in Bolivia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sucre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the south, the costly Battle of Bomboná and <b>Sucre</b>’s victory at the Battle of Pichincha in 1822 secured Quito.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The region saw some of the earliest risings for self-government, at <b>Chuquisaca</b> and La Paz in 1809, and its rural guerrilla bands, the republiquetas, fought the royalists for fifteen years, but Spanish armies based in Peru held the main cities until the end of the wars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-bolivia">Republic of Bolivia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>An assembly in <b>Chuquisaca</b> proclaimed the independence of Upper Peru, naming the new republic after Simón Bolívar.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-6">6 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the centuries after Tiwanaku's collapse, the Qaraqara and Charka formed a federation of peoples in the southern highlands of modern Bolivia, around Potosí and <b>Chuquisaca</b>, around 1200.</blockquote><a href="/state/qaraqara-charka-confederation">Qaraqara-Charka Confederation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1559 the crown created a Royal Audiencia at <b>La Plata</b>, also called Chuquisaca and now Sucre, to govern the region of Charcas, or Upper Peru, within the Viceroyalty of Peru.</blockquote><a href="/state/royal-audiencia-of-charcas">Royal Audiencia of Charcas</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sucre's</b> victory at Pichincha in 1822 brought Quito into Gran Colombia.</blockquote><a href="/state/royal-audiencia-of-quito">Royal Audiencia of Quito</a></li></ul></section>
